fix:
In pageAgent (ui.html), the fix has five parts.

1) Detection: iconInfo(el, cs) returns null, or one of
- {kind: 'fa', name, style}
- {kind: 'ms', name, variant, fill}
- {kind: 'glyph', ch, family, weight}
Each result also carries pseudo: '::before' / '::after' / null.

How detection works:
- For p of ['::before', '::after']:
  - `const b = css(el, p)`; then `m = /^"([\s\S]*)"$/.exec(b.content)`.
  - If m is null, skip. Unescape it; Chrome returns the literal character.
  - Take `const cp = text.codePointAt(0)`. It is an icon when text has 1-2 code points AND either:
    - cp is in U+E000..U+F8FF or ≥ U+F0000, or
    - b.fontFamily matches /Font Awesome|bootstrap-icons|remixicon|icomoon|tabler|Phosphor|Material Icons|boxicons|lucide/i.
  - Font Awesome: if /Font Awesome/i matches b.fontFamily, take the name from el.classList. Use the first fa-* class that is not a modifier:
    `/^fa-(solid|regular|brands|light|thin|duotone|sharp|fw|xs|sm|lg|xl|2xs|2xl|\dx|10x|spin|spin-pulse|spin-reverse|pulse|beat|beat-fade|bounce|fade|flip|shake|border|inverse|pull-left|pull-right|rotate-\d+|rotate-by|flip-horizontal|flip-vertical|flip-both|stack|stack-1x|stack-2x|ul|li)$/`
  - FA style: 'brands' if /Brands/i matches b.fontFamily, or the class is fa-brands/fab; else 'regular' if parseInt(b.fontWeight) < 900; else 'solid'.
- Ligature check (el's own text, no element children): `/Material (Icons|Symbols)/i.test(cs.fontFamily) && /^[a-z0-9_]+$/.test(text.trim())`. That gives:
  - variant from the family: outlined / rounded / sharp
  - `fill = /'FILL'\s+1/.test(cs.fontVariationSettings)`

2) Placeholder node, created synchronously during walk.
- Size and box: s = parseFloat(cs.fontSize) (from b for the pseudo case). Take cb = contentBox(el, cs). For 'fa', w = s * vbW / vbH once the SVG is known; until then use s. For ms, w = h = s. Centre the box in cb: x = cb.x + (cb.w - w)/2, same for y.
- Colour: color = parseColor(b ? b.color : cs.color).
- Push the node `{type: 'SVG', name: 'icon · ' + name, x, y, w, h, svg: null, _icon: {url, color, fallback}}`, and record it in S.icons (reset in extract()).
- URLs:
  - FA: `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@fortawesome/fontawesome-free@6.5.1/svgs/${style}/${name}.svg`
  - MS: `https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/i/short-term/release/materialsymbols${variant}/${name}/${fill ? 'fill1' : 'default'}/24px.svg`
  - Material Icons (the older font): same pattern with materialsymbols${variant}, which keeps the same names.
- Fallback: render a canvas raster immediately while the page font is loaded.
  - Create a canvas of round(w*4) x round(h*4).
  - `ctx.font = `${style} ${weight} ${s*4}px "${family}"``; fillStyle = color; textAlign = 'center'; textBaseline = 'middle'; then fillText(ch, cw/2, ch/2).
  - Store `fallback = {type: 'IMAGE', ref: imageRef(canvas.toDataURL('image/png'), null), scaleMode: 'FIT'}`.
  - For ligatures, draw the ligature word; Chrome applies liga on canvas. Canvas ignores font-variation-settings, so FILL is lost here, which is acceptable for a fallback.

3) Wiring in walk(): call `const icon = iconInfo(el, cs)` right after `visual` is computed and before `isTextBlock`.
- If icon is set and el has no other non-whitespace content (ignoring the ligature text itself): kids = [iconNode] (shifted to rect.left/top).
  - If !visual.has, return the icon node itself, positioned relative to ox/oy, plus common.
  - Otherwise wrap it as frameNode(el, box, visual, common, [iconNode]) and call tryAutoLayout.
  - This branch must come before the `plain && !kids.length` early return.
- If el has text AND a pseudo icon (e.g. `<a class="link-icon">Docs</a>` with ::before): build the frame children as [iconNode, textLayer] for ::before, or [textLayer, iconNode] for ::after.
  - For that text layer, measure the text with textGeometry on the text nodes only; Range rects exclude pseudo content.

4) isTextBlock(): inside scan(), for each element child c (and for el itself before scanning), return false if iconInfo(c, css(c)) is non-null. The parent then goes through childrenOf(), and the inline `<i>` gets its own walk(). This fixes `<button><i class="bi bi-check"></i> Save</button>` and `<p><i class="fa fa-star"></i> 4.9</p>`, which today merge into text and drop the icon.

5) Async resolution in the message handler: right after `const root = extract(opts)` and BEFORE `const list = [...S.images.values()]`, add:
```
await Promise.all(S.icons.map(async (n) => {
  let svg = null;
  try {
    const r = await Promise.race([fetch(n._icon.url), sleep(4000).then(() => null)]);
    if (r && r.ok) svg = await r.text();
  } catch (e) {}
  if (svg && /^\s*<svg[\s>]/.test(svg)) {
    const d = new DOMParser().parseFromString(svg, 'image/svg+xml').documentElement;
    const vb = (d.getAttribute('viewBox') || '0 0 24 24').split(/[\s,]+/).map(Number);
    if (n._icon.kind === 'fa') {
      const nw = n.h * vb[2] / vb[3];
      n.x = r2(n.x + (n.w - nw) / 2);
      n.w = r2(nw);
    }
    d.setAttribute('width', n.w);
    d.setAttribute('height', n.h);
    d.setAttribute('fill', rgbaString(n._icon.color));
    // strip comments
    n.svg = new XMLSerializer().serializeToString(d);
  } else {
    n.type = 'RECT';
    n.fills = [n._icon.fallback];
    delete n.svg;
  }
  delete n._icon;
}));
```
If opts.images is off and the fetch fails, fall back to a solid-colour RECT the size of the glyph rather than nothing.

code.js needs no change: it already handles SVG and RECT+IMAGE nodes. Keep it ES2017. The SVG root must carry explicit width/height, since createNodeFromSvg sizing depends on them.

Harness note (separate, kind=harness): dev/render.html requests every family with `:ital,wght@0,100..900;1,100..900`. For Material Symbols that request fails and the text falls back to a serif font, which is why the ligature words look wider than they would in Figma.

Put a paint-order model in ui.html walk()/childrenOf(). Do it by lifting nodes while the tree is built, not with a separate pass afterwards.

1) In walk(el, ...), after cs is known:
   pos = cs.position !== 'static';
   zAuto = cs.zIndex === 'auto';
   z = zAuto ? 0 : (parseInt(cs.zIndex, 10) || 0);
   parentDisp = el.parentElement ? css(el.parentElement).display : '';
   flexItem = /flex|grid/.test(parentDisp);
   isSC = (pos && !zAuto) || cs.position === 'fixed' || cs.position === 'sticky' || (flexItem && !zAuto) || opacity < 1 || cs.transform !== 'none' || cs.filter !== 'none' || (cs.backdropFilter && cs.backdropFilter !== 'none') || cs.isolation === 'isolate' || cs.mixBlendMode !== 'normal' || (cs.clipPath && cs.clipPath !== 'none') || /paint|layout|strict|content/.test(cs.contain || '') || /transform|opacity|filter/.test(cs.willChange || '');
   Store the result on every node the element produces: node._paint = { z, pos: pos || (flexItem && !zAuto), isSC }. Do this in leaf(), the text-block return and frameNode(). When the "plain wrapper with one kid" shortcut fires and the wrapper itself had a non-auto z, copy the wrapper's z and pos onto the kid.

2) Lift out of non-stacking parents. In walk(), after `const kids = childrenOf(el, rect.left, rect.top)`:
   if (!isSC) {
     lifted = kids.filter(k => k.absolute && k._paint && k._paint.z !== 0);
     kids = the remaining kids;
     for each k in lifted: k.x += box.x; k.y += box.y   (convert to the parent's coordinates, as the one-kid shortcut does)
   }
   Build the frame from the remaining kids, then return [...lifted.filter(z < 0), f, ...lifted.filter(z > 0)].
   - Negative-z descendants bubble up until an element that creates a stacking context keeps them. extract() is the root and keeps everything left over.
   - Occlusion by any intermediate opaque background then follows naturally, because the frames come after the lifted node. Nothing needs to be dropped.
   - If the non-stacking element clips (visual.clip) and the lifted node lies outside its box, wrap the lifted node in an empty clip frame with the element's box (fills [], clip true) so it is still clipped.
   - Only lift absolute or fixed nodes. An in-flow flex or grid item with z-index stays where it is, so auto layout is not broken.

3) Order inside the stacking context. In walk() for elements with isSC, and in extract() for the root, stable-sort children before tryAutoLayout with
   key(k) = !k.absolute ? 0 : (z < 0 ? -1e6 + z : z > 0 ? 1e6 + z : 1)
   (absolute positioned z-auto/0 nodes get key 1).
   In-flow nodes all share key 0, so their relative order is untouched and Auto Layout order stays correct. Absolute nodes with z-auto still paint above earlier non-positioned in-flow siblings, which matches the browser. Negative-z nodes move before all in-flow nodes but stay after the frame's own fills, which is correct because the element is the stacking context. Positive-z nodes go last, in ascending z.
   To mirror the browser more closely, also give positioned in-flow nodes (position:relative, z auto) key 0.5. But only sort them when the parent ends up with no auto layout (check after tryAutoLayout). If the parent does get auto layout, leave in-flow order alone.

4) Strip the temporary _paint field from each node before postMessage, or ignore it in code.js. code.js needs no changes: it already appends children in array order and sets layoutPositioning='ABSOLUTE' for absolute kids of auto-layout frames.

5) When ::before/::after are materialized (the separate finding), give them the same _paint data. Insert ::before as the first child and ::after as the last child in DOM terms, then let steps 2 and 3 place them. With that, .hl::after (z-index -1, no stacking ancestor) lifts to the root and ends up under .hero's fill (invisible, as in the browser). The before:-z-10 gradient border in gen-tw-controls lifts out of the card and lands just before the card frame, so it is visible only as the 2px rim if some ancestor up to its stacking context has no opaque fill. In gen-tw-controls it is hidden, matching orig.png.

