**ui.html (pageAgent)**

1. Add a helper that returns the clockwise rotation in degrees, or null.
```js
function rotationOf(cs){
  let m = new DOMMatrix();
  const r = cs.rotate;                       // '45deg' | 'z 45deg' | 'x y z 45deg' | 'none'
  if (r && r !== 'none') {
    const p = r.trim().split(/\s+/);
    if (p.length === 1 || (p.length === 2 && p[0] === 'z') || (p.length === 4 && +p[0] === 0 && +p[1] === 0)) {
      m = m.rotate(angleDeg(p[p.length - 1]));   // angleDeg handles deg/rad/turn/grad
    } else return null;                           // 3D rotation: leave as bbox
  }
  if (cs.scale && cs.scale !== 'none') {
    const s = cs.scale.split(/\s+/).map(parseFloat);
    m = m.scale(s[0], s.length > 1 ? s[1] : s[0]);
  }
  if (cs.transform && cs.transform !== 'none') m = m.multiply(new DOMMatrix(cs.transform));
  if (!m.is2D) return null;
  const sx = Math.hypot(m.a, m.b), sy = Math.hypot(m.c, m.d);
  if (Math.abs(m.a * m.c + m.b * m.d) > 1e-3 * sx * sy) return null;  // skew: keep current bbox behaviour
  const deg = Math.atan2(m.b, m.a) * 180 / Math.PI;
  return Math.abs(deg) < 0.05 ? null : deg;
}
```
The CSS `translate` property can be ignored here, because the true centre is taken from the bounding rect below.

2. In `walk()`, right after the visibility and opacity checks:
   - Compute `deg = rotationOf(cs)`.
   - If `deg` is set:
     - Take `r0 = el.getBoundingClientRect()` and the true centre `cx = (r0.left + r0.right) / 2`, `cy = (r0.top + r0.bottom) / 2`.
     - Save the inline values of `transform`, `rotate`, `scale` and `translate`, then set each with `el.style.setProperty(p, 'none', 'important')`.
     - Inside `try`, run the normal `walk` body. With the transform neutralized, `rect` is the untransformed layout box (this works for SVG too), and `childrenOf(el, rect.left, rect.top)`, Range rects and `svgNode` all measure in the element's local, unrotated space.
     - In `finally`, restore the saved inline values.
   - Transforms do not affect layout, so nothing else on the page moves.
   - For each node returned:
     - If a wrapper was lifted (more than one node, or a plain single child), skip the rotation: do not lift through a rotated element.
     - Otherwise set `node.x = r2(cx - node.w / 2 - ox)`, `node.y = r2(cy - node.h / 2 - oy)`, `node.rotation = r2(deg)` and `node.absolute = true`. CSS flow ignores transforms, while Figma auto layout would use the rotated bbox, so the node must not take part in auto layout.
   - `fitLayout` must already skip absolute kids.
3. Pseudo-elements (separate prerequisite): when `::before`/`::after` support is added, materialize each one as a real temporary `<span>` that carries the pseudo's computed style, including `transform`/`rotate`. This rotation path then applies unchanged, and check marks and chevrons come out right.

**code.js (ES2017)**

In `buildNode`, after `place(node, n, parent)`:
```js
if (typeof n.rotation === 'number' && n.rotation) {
  if ('layoutMode' in parent && parent.layoutMode !== 'NONE') node.layoutPositioning = 'ABSOLUTE';
  var t = n.rotation * Math.PI / 180, c = Math.cos(t), s = Math.sin(t);
  var w = node.width, h = node.height;        // real size (text may be auto-sized)
  var cx = n.x + n.w / 2, cy = n.y + n.h / 2;
  node.relativeTransform = [[c, -s, cx - (c * w / 2 - s * h / 2)], [s, c, cy - (s * w / 2 + c * h / 2)]];
}
```
This is equivalent to Figma `rotation = -deg`, because CSS rotates clockwise and Figma rotation is counter-clockwise. Setting the full matrix also places the node correctly.

